// ===========================================================================
// 49-aquarius-udisks.rules — mount/unmount a removable drive with no password
// ===========================================================================
// PLAIN ENGLISH
//
// polkit is the part of Linux that decides "is this person allowed to do this,
// and do they need to type a password first?". This file is one narrow rule:
// the person actually sitting at this computer may mount, unmount and eject a
// REMOVABLE drive — a USB stick, an SD card, an external SSD — without a
// password, exactly like on a Mac.
//
// It is what makes two things work with no prompt:
//   * a drive mounting by itself when you plug it in
//     (/usr/libexec/aquarius-automount asks udisks2 to do it), and
//   * the dock's right-click "Eject / Unmount"
//     (the shell runs `udisksctl unmount` / `udisksctl power-off`).
//
// It is loaded before Fedora's own udisks rules because its filename sorts
// before "50-" — so for the actions below, our answer is the one that stands.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS IS SAFE — AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two things keep this tight, and both matter:
//
// 1. WHO. `subject.local && subject.active` means "the login session that owns
//    the screen and keyboard right now". Not someone connected over SSH
//    (not local), not a session that has been switched away from (not active),
//    and never the login screen (that is a system account, handled below).
//    A password-free mount is only ever granted to the person physically here.
//
// 2. WHAT. udisks2 uses a DIFFERENT polkit action depending on the kind of
//    device:
//
//        filesystem-mount          a REMOVABLE / hotplugged drive
//        filesystem-mount-system   a SYSTEM-INTERNAL disk (the boot drive, an
//                                  extra internal disk, anything in /etc/fstab)
//
//    This rule answers ONLY for the removable set — filesystem-mount,
//    eject-media and power-off-drive. It says nothing about
//    filesystem-mount-system, so a request to mount an internal disk falls
//    through to Fedora's default, which still asks for an administrator
//    password. That is the whole point: convenient for the drives you carry,
//    unchanged for the disk the operating system lives on.
//
// It also does NOT grant filesystem-unmount-others. Unmounting a drive that
// YOU mounted never needs a password in the first place, and the automount
// agent mounts as you — so the dock's unmount is already password-free without
// it. Granting the "others" variant would let you unmount a mount that belongs
// to a different user with no password, which is not something we need.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHECK IT DID WHAT IT SAYS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The build (build_files/76-automount.sh) and CI both grep this file to prove
// it still names subject.active and subject.local, still returns YES for
// filesystem-mount, and still says NOTHING about filesystem-mount-system — so a
// future edit cannot quietly turn this into a blanket "mount anything with no
// password".
// ===========================================================================

polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    // Only the person sitting at this machine, in the session that owns the
    // screen right now. Anything else is left to polkit's defaults.
    if (!(subject.local && subject.active)) {
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
    }

    // The removable-drive actions, and ONLY these. Note there is no
    // "org.freedesktop.udisks2.filesystem-mount-system" here on purpose:
    // mounting an internal/system disk must still ask for a password.
    if (action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.filesystem-mount" ||
        action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.eject-media" ||
        action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.power-off-drive") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }

    // Everything else: not our business, use the system default.
    return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});
